---
title: Filesystems
order: 2
description: Creating, checking, and tuning filesystems
tags: [disk, filesystem, mkfs, fsck, ext4, btrfs, xfs, fat, tune2fs, resize2fs]
---

Commands for creating, checking, repairing, and tuning filesystems. Always unmount before running fsck or resize operations unless the tool explicitly supports live operation.

@text
Create filesystems with mkfs.

@code bash
# ext4 — general-purpose Linux filesystem
sudo mkfs.ext4 /dev/sda1

# ext4 with label
sudo mkfs.ext4 -L rootfs /dev/sda1

# ext4 tuned for large storage (fewer inodes, larger blocks)
sudo mkfs.ext4 -T largefile /dev/sda1

# btrfs — copy-on-write filesystem with snapshots
sudo mkfs.btrfs /dev/sda1

# btrfs with label
sudo mkfs.btrfs -L data /dev/sda1

# btrfs on multiple devices (RAID1 metadata, RAID0 data)
sudo mkfs.btrfs -m raid1 -d raid0 /dev/sda1 /dev/sdb1

# XFS — high-performance filesystem
sudo mkfs.xfs /dev/sda1

# XFS with label
sudo mkfs.xfs -L fastdata /dev/sda1

# FAT32 — for EFI partitions and USB drives
sudo mkfs.fat -F 32 /dev/sda1

# FAT32 with label (max 11 chars)
sudo mkfs.fat -F 32 -n EFI /dev/sda1

# exFAT — large file support on removable media
sudo mkfs.exfat -n USBDRIVE /dev/sda1

@text
Check and repair filesystems with fsck. The filesystem must be unmounted.

@code bash
# Check ext4 filesystem (interactive repair)
sudo fsck.ext4 /dev/sda1

# Automatically repair without prompts
sudo fsck.ext4 -y /dev/sda1

# Check without making changes (dry run)
sudo fsck.ext4 -n /dev/sda1

# Force check even if filesystem appears clean
sudo fsck.ext4 -f /dev/sda1

# Check XFS (xfs_repair replaces fsck.xfs)
sudo xfs_repair /dev/sda1

# XFS dry run
sudo xfs_repair -n /dev/sda1

# Check btrfs
sudo btrfs check /dev/sda1

# btrfs check with repair (use with caution)
sudo btrfs check --repair /dev/sda1

# Check FAT filesystem
sudo fsck.fat -a /dev/sda1

@text
Tune ext4 filesystem parameters with tune2fs.

@code bash
# Show filesystem info
sudo tune2fs -l /dev/sda1

# Set filesystem label
sudo tune2fs -L mydata /dev/sda1

# Set reserved block percentage (default 5%, lower for data drives)
sudo tune2fs -m 1 /dev/sda1

# Disable automatic fsck interval (check count)
sudo tune2fs -c 0 /dev/sda1

# Disable automatic fsck interval (time-based)
sudo tune2fs -i 0 /dev/sda1

# Enable features (e.g., dir_index for faster directory lookups)
sudo tune2fs -O dir_index /dev/sda1

# Set mount count before next forced fsck
sudo tune2fs -c 30 /dev/sda1

@text
Resize ext4 filesystems with resize2fs. Grow online or shrink offline.

@code bash
# Grow ext4 to fill partition (online, filesystem mounted)
sudo resize2fs /dev/sda1

# Shrink ext4 to specific size (must be unmounted)
sudo umount /dev/sda1
sudo e2fsck -f /dev/sda1
sudo resize2fs /dev/sda1 50G

# Resize to exact block count
sudo resize2fs /dev/sda1 13107200

@text
XFS can only be grown, never shrunk, and must be mounted.

@code bash
# Grow XFS to fill available space (by mountpoint)
sudo xfs_growfs /mnt/data

@text
Btrfs resize (must be mounted).

@code bash
# Grow btrfs to fill device
sudo btrfs filesystem resize max /mnt/data

# Shrink btrfs by 10 GiB
sudo btrfs filesystem resize -10G /mnt/data

# Resize to exact size
sudo btrfs filesystem resize 100G /mnt/data
